            <div xml:id="d932">

                <pb facs="1051" n="947" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg947"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">823.00 Revolutions/331: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Burdett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lima</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-07-12T09:00:00-05:00">July 12, 1932—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>103. Embassy learns authentic source Trujillo only partially occupied by
                    Government July 11, 4 p.m. and street fighting and sniping continued. Total
                    casualties exceed 100.</p>
                <p>Only Americans thought to be the city are H. A. Jaynes, United States Department
                    of Agriculture, and Arthur Erickson, Pentecostal missionary.</p>
                <p>Situation in Lima normal.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Burdett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
